# Highly Air‐Stable Single‐Crystalline β‐CsPbI<sub>3</sub> Nanorods: A Platform for Inverted Perovskite Solar Cells.

## abstract
The synthesis of single‐crystalline β‐CsPbI3 perovskite nanorods (NRs) using a colloidal process is reported, exhibiting their improved photostability under 45–55% humidity. The crystal structure of CsPbI3 NRs films is investigated using Rietveld refined X‐ray diffraction (XRD) patterns to determine crystallographic parameters and the phase transformation from orthorhombic (γ‐CsPbI3) to tetragonal (β‐CsPbI3) on annealing at 150 °C. Atomic resolution transmission electron microscopy images are utilized to determine the probable atomic distribution of Cs, Pb, and I atoms in a single β‐phase CsPbI3 NR, in agreement with the XRD structure and selected area electron diffraction pattern, indicating the growth of single crystalline β‐CsPbI3 NR. The calculation of the electronic band structure of tetragonal β‐CsPbI3 using density functional theory (DFT) reveals a direct transition with a lower band gap and a higher absorption coefficient in the solar spectrum, as compared to its γ‐phase. An air‐stable (45–55% humidity) inverted perovskite solar cell, employing β‐CsPbI3 NRs without any encapsulation, yields an efficiency of 7.3% with 78% enhancement over the γ‐phase, showing its potential for future low cost photovoltaic devices.

## Highly Air-Stable Single-Crystalline β-CsPbI3 Nanorods: A Platform for Inverted Perovskite Solar Cells
The synthesis of single-crystalline ${\beta}{\tt c s P b l}_{3}$ perovskite nanorods (NRs) using a colloidal process is reported, exhibiting their improved photostability under $45\text{\textperthousand}$ humidity. The crystal structure of $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}$ NRs films is investigated using Rietveld refined $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{x}}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) patterns to determine crystallographic parameters and the phase transformation from orthorhombic $(\gamma\mathsf{c c s P b l}_{3})$ to tetragonal $(\beta\cdot\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3})$ on annealing at $\mathsf{150^{\circ}C}$ . Atomic resolution transmission electron microscopy images are utilized to determine the probable atomic distribution of Cs, Pb, and I atoms in a single $\beta\mathrm{.}$ phase $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}$ NR, in agreement with the XRD structure and selected area electron diffraction pattern, indicating the growth of single crystalline ${\beta}{\mathsf{-C s P b l}}_{3}$ NR. The calculation of the electronic band structure of tetragonal ${\beta}{\mathsf{c}}{\mathsf{s P b l}}_{3}$ using density functional theory (DFT) reveals a direct transition with a lower band gap and a higher absorption coefficient in the solar spectrum, as compared to its γ-phase. An air-stable $(45-55\%$ humidity) inverted perovskite solar cell, employing ${\beta}{\mathsf{c}}{\mathsf{s P b l}}_{3}$ NRs without any encapsulation, yields an efficiency of $7.3\%$ with $78\%$ enhancement over the γ-phase, showing its potential for future low cost photovoltaic devices.
## 1. Introduction
All inorganic perovskites $\mathrm{(CsPb{X_{3}}}$ : $\Chi=\operatorname{Cl}$ , Br, and I) are very promising for the development of next-generation optoelectronic and photovoltaic (PV) devices.[1] From application point of view, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ is one of the most important inorganic perovskites due to its high diffusion length $(>1\upmu\mathrm{m})$ , high absorption coefficient $(10^{5}\mathrm{cm})$ , excellent charge transport properties, and high photo­stability.[2–5] $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ exists in four phases; $\alpha\mathrm{.}$ cubic (black color) is only stable at a high temperature $(>320^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , $\beta$ tetragonal and γ-orthorhombic phases are stable at low temperatures, and $\delta$ orthorhombic (yellow color) is the nonperovskite phase that is an optically inactive semiconductor. It has been predicted that the crystal structure of tetragonal $\beta$ -phase is more stable than the cubic $\alpha$ phase.[6,7] However, all stable phases can convert into a yellow non-perovskite one on prolonged exposure to humid condition. Therefore, the realization of long-term stability of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ , especially in high humidity and temperature, is a challenging task.[8,9] Recently, Wang et  al.[6] have reported the synthesis of tetragonal ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ thin films, which exhibited higher stability and a more suitable band gap, giving rise to photovoltaic conversion efficiency (PCE) of $15\%$ without any surface passivation.
In addition to thin films, different nanocrystals of perovskites have been investigated extensively in recent years due to their size-dependent properties useful for optical devices with enhanced performance and stability.[10–13] However the fundamental atomic structures imaging of all inorganic halide perovskites in real-space is difficult to extract by atomic resolution transmission electron microscopy (TEM) because of the sensitivity of their structures to the destructive electron beam. As a result, such atomic resolution imaging[14–16] is very important for the understanding of novel and unique properties of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ . $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ nanocrystals have a tunable band gap (from ${\approx}1.7$ to ${\approx}2.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ ), excellent photoluminescence, and outstanding light harvesting properties.[17–20] Therefore, they have triggered wide and exciting applications in high-performance optoelectronic devices.[21–24] Besides the photodetectors and light-emitting devices (LEDs), they possess a great potential for PV applications requiring them to be synthesized using a low cost process. Among different shapes, 1D nanostructures (nanorods (NRs)/nanowires) are potentially attractive due to their high crystalline, large surface-to-volume ratio, and capability of having both horizontal and vertical device configurations. To date, several groups[25–27] have developed techniques for synthesizing 1D $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ nanocrystals for electronic and optoelectronic devices but all studies till date are focused on $\gamma$ and $\alpha{\mathrm{-CsPbI}}_{3}$ nanocrystals or quantum dots-based hightemperature conventional (n-i-p architecture) perovskite solar cells. However, the fabrication of ${\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ NR- based inverted perovskite solar cells (p-i-n architecture) is challenging and, to the best of our knowledge, not yet reported so far.
Herein, we first report a novel, low-cost colloidal synthesis of high-quality single-crystalline ${\beta\mathrm{{\cdotCsPbI}}_{3}}$ NRs thin film that is kinetically stable at room temperature in presence of humidity $(45-55\%)$ and showing remarkable stability up to 15 days without any passivation. Rietveld refinement of X-ray diffraction (XRD) results confirmed that an as-deposited thin film has an orthorhombic $(\gamma{\mathsf{C s P b I}}_{3})$ phase, which is converted to a tetragonal $(\beta\mathrm{{\cdotCsPbI}}_{3})$ one on annealing at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 min. The calculation of electronic band structure and density of states (DOS) using the refined structure revealed that $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ annealed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ NRs indeed have a direct band gap with tetragonal phase. We visualized for the first time, the probable atomic arrangements of Cs, $\mathrm{Pb}$ , and I atoms in a single ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ NR by using atomic resolution TEM micrograph and selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern indicates a high-quality single crystalline $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ NR. To demonstrate the proof of concept, air-stable ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ NR-based inverted perovskite solar cells (IPSCs), without any use of glovebox or encapsulation, having a moderately high efficiency $(7.3\%)$ is also reported.
In this work, single-crystalline $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ NRs were synthesized using a cheap, colloidal synthesis process. Detailed synthesis process has been described in the previous work.[28] Figure  1a represents a freshly prepared $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ solution that was stable for more than 90 days. The freshly prepared solution was deposited on glass substrates by spin coating with a spinning rate of $1000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $40\mathrm{~s~}$ followed by annealing at different temperatures for a constant duration of $3\ \mathrm{min}$ , as shown in Figure  1b. The as-deposited film was stable only for a few days but showed a significant improvement in stability on annealing at an optimized temperature ${\approx}150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The uniformity and quality of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ NRs films were found to degrade on annealing at a higher temperature $(\geq175~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , as shown in Figure S1, Supporting Information. Figure  1c schematically depicts a proposed thermal phase relation of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ based on our observations and reported work.[29]
## 2. Results and Discussions
To determine the phase of as-deposited and annealed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ films, we have performed XRD measurements at room temperature and the pattern for a fresh as-deposited $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ film is shown in Figure S2, Supporting Information. XRD peaks located at $2\theta=14.24^{\circ}$ , $20.34^{\circ}$ , $24.33^{\circ}$ , $26.37^{\circ}$ , $30.68^{\circ}$ , $32.61^{\circ}$ , $33.48^{\circ}$ , and $34.82^{\circ}$ represent the crystallographic planes of (110), (112), (022), (122), (221), (310), (311), and (024), respectively, for an orthorhombic phase (γ-phase) of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ in agreement with reported results.[17,30] On optimized annealing at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure  1d), the peaks at $30.68^{\circ}$ , $32.61^{\circ}$ , $33.48^{\circ}$ , and $34.82^{\circ}$ are suppressed and the main peak is shifted to $2\theta=14.85^{\circ}$ (shown in Figure S3, Supporting Information) corresponding to (110) plane. This high intense peak represents a very good crystalline quality of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ NRs with the growth along [110] direction. To determine the phase and space group of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ thin films, we performed the Rietveld refinement by using a FullProf program. It is evident that most of the as-deposited XRD peaks match with the orthorhombic phase having space group Pnma, no. 62. On the other hand, the annealed film shows a mixture of tetragonal (space group P4/mbm, no. 127) and orthorhombic phases (space group Pnma, no. 62). Using Rietveld refinement, we have calculated the tetragonal to orthorhombic phase ratio using double phase fitting of $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ annealed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ film to be 10:1. The obtained crystallographic parameters are summarized in Table ST1, Supporting Information. Using calculated lattice parameters, we have drawn 3D crystal structure and generated XRD pattern of $\gamma$ and $\beta\mathrm{.}$ phases of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ using a 3D visualization VESTA program (Figure S4, Supporting Information). The structure shown in the inset of Figure 1d is in excellent agreement with our experimental high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) analysis to be discussed later. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) has been carried out to investigate the effect of annealing on chemical composition of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ . XPS survey scans of asdeposited and annealed samples are shown in Figure 1e, with the figure insets showing deconvoluted Cs 3d, I 3d, and Pb 4f core level spectra corresponding to the doublets of Cs $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ Cs $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ , I $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ , $\mathrm{~I~}3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ , Pb $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ , and Pb $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ electrons for the annealed sample.[31,32] Neither any additional peaks due to impurity phases nor any significant shift of the binding energy peak for Cs 3d, Pb 4f, and I 3d (Figure S5, Supporting Information) electrons are detected on annealing the sample at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This indicates that the change of crystal structure (from orthorhombic to tetragonal) improves only the material stability without any change in the chemical composition of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ .
The surface morphology of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ NRs films has been studied using field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM). From the micrographs (Figure S6a,b, Supporting Information), it is observed that as-deposited and annealed films $(150^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ are continuous and composed of 1D structures of nonuniform size. It may be noted that the diameter and length of grown nanostructures vary from 20 to $200\mathrm{nm}$ and $200\mathrm{nm}$ to $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , respectively. A relatively higher aspect ratio (diameter-tolength ratio) of 1D structure indicates that the grown samples are indeed nanorods, rather than nanowires with extremely long length or very low aspect ratio. All NRs are homogeneously distributed over the surface though the as-deposited film appears to be rough, which may lead to the increased recombination of charge carriers. On the other hand, the annealed film exhibits better uniformity and smoother surface that are desirable to minimize charge carrier recombination. For a closer look into the micro-structural properties of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ NRs, we have studied TEM analysis as shown in Figure S6c,d,
Supporting Information. TEM micrographs reveal that NRs become smoother and perfectly aligned in a particular direction after annealing. HRTEM micrographs presented in Figure 2a,b show the lattice fringes of NRs, providing additional information on the growth direction and crystallinity. In the annealed sample (Figure 2b), sharper lattice fringes indicate an improved crystalline quality with reduced grain boundaries, as compared to the as-deposited sample in Figure 2a. The fast Fourier transform (FFT) patterns (Figure  2e,f) are obtained from the red dotted squares on HRTEM images of Figure 2a,b and indexed properly. The crystal planes reveal that the as-deposited $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ NR has an orthorhombic phase viewed from [201] zone axis and annealed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ NR exhibits a tetragonal phase viewed from [102] zone axis. However, a few additional diffraction spots not belonging to the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ are also observed in Figure  2e,f. These additional spots are attributed to the presence of a finite amount of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and CsI, which may be formed due to the surface decomposition of the sample at a high electron beam dose.[12] The decomposition of the sample at a high dose also gives rise to a few bubbles in HRTEM images of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ NRs. Bubbles-free HRTEM images recorded for as-deposited and annealed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ samples before any degradation are presented in Figure S7, Supporting Information.
To further investigate the precise arrangement of atoms in a single $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ NRs using a “polyhedral” model, we have used magnified atomic resolution images shown in Figure 2c,d processed by a Digital Micrograph software. In as-deposited sample (Figure  2c), it is difficult to identify the atoms due to lattice distortion,[33–35] which is in well-agreement with the 3D crystal structure shown in Figure $\mathtt{S4a}$ , Supporting Information. In our synthesis, we have not used any kind of polar solvent for the lattice distortion. The annealing resulted in the phase transition and stability of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ perovskite by suppressing the lattice distortion. XRD patterns presented in Figure S3, Supporting Information, showing a large peak shift for the annealed film discussed earlier, corroborate thermally induced phase transition of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ . This is in agreement with previous reports on thermal phase relation of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ using synchrotronbased, grazing incidence and wide-angle X-ray scattering analy­ses along with the theoretical support.[29,30] In our synthesis, we have used olive oil as a substitution of oleic acid (OA). Compared to OA, olive oil has better stability in air and being edible can be obtained easily from a supermarket, as reported by Xu et al.[36] Sun et al.[33] reported that as-synthesized $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ colloidal solution can be stabilized by the ligands. However, these ligands are not favorable for efficient charge transport in the material. So, unwanted ligands (fatty acids or oleylamine) or by-products have been eliminated by centrifugation of the solution several times using excess hexane. However, even after repeated centrifugation process, there may be a trace amount of oleylamine or fatty acids, which will be helpful to improve the stability of the final product in ambient condition. While remaining in the solution phase for a few hours, highly concentrated $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ nanocrystals are attached together side-by-side and self-assembled in lateral direction to form uneven NRs with a lattice distortion in as-synthesized sample.[33,37] We calculated the lattice distortion using the VESTA structure, as shown in Figure S4a,b, Supporting Information. In as-deposited $\gamma{\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ NRs, the I-Pb-I bond angle of $\mathrm{PbI}_{6}$ octahedra is $88.44^{\circ}$ , which deviates by $1.56^{\circ}$ from the original value of $90^{\circ}$ . Whereas, in the ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ NRs, I-Pb-I bond angle is exactly $90^{\circ}$ .[38] Therefore, a clear lattice distortion is observed in the $\gamma{\mathsf{C s P b I}}_{3}$ NRs whereas ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ NRs has a distortion free $\mathrm{PbI}_{6}$ octahedra. Finally, on annealing at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , we obtained a highly oriented along [110] direction and high crystalline ${\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ NRs with distortion free unit cell by minimizing the surface energy.[39] Meanwhile, the orientation force (dipole−dipole attraction) pulls atoms inside the unit cell leading to the minimization of lattice distortion of $\mathrm{PbI}_{6}$ octahedra, which are well-visualized in the XRD structure (Figure S4b, Supporting Information). Once the atomic arrangements are perfectly ordered, the unit cell volume is minimized resulting in the long-term stability of ${\beta\mathrm{{\cdotCsPbI}}_{3}}$ NRs even under humid conditions. The experimentally extracted unitcell volume of the annealed sample from XRD data is almost half of the value for as-deposited one shown in Table ST1, Supporting Information. Through our analysis, we observe that the annealing process not only reduces the ligand density but also significantly affects the stability of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ NRs.
For detailed analysis of the atomic distribution, we have performed XRD measurements and calculated all crystallographic parameters by Rietveld refinement analysis. Using these parameters, we have drawn a crystal structure of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ by 3D visualization VESTA software shown in Figure  3c. From this structure, we have calculated the distance between two atoms $(\mathrm{Pb{-}P b}=8.49\ \dot{\$ Å, $\mathrm{Cs-Cs}=8.49\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , and $\mathrm{I-I}=4.17\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). On the other hand, from HRTEM (shown in Figure 3a) image, we also calculated the atomic distance by the Digital Micrograph software, as shown in the right panel of Figure  3b. The distance between two atoms derived from XRD analysis is very close to that calculated from HRTEM image, as shown in the bottom panel of Figure  3b. Therefore, Figure  3b represents the pro­ bable atomic distribution of Cs, Pb, and I atoms in a single ${\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ NRs based on both XRD and HRTEM data. The experimentally (XRD) extracted tetragonal ${\beta\mathrm{{csPbI}}_{3}}$ (Pb−I−Pb bond angle $162.71^{\circ}\mathrm{~.~}$ ) phase shows a lower octahedral to octahedral inclination in the lattice as compared to the orthorhombic $\gamma{\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ $({\mathrm{Pb-I-Pb}}$ bond angle $153.20^{\circ}\}$ phase, which is attributed to the antibonding and bonding character of the orbital $\mathrm{\sfPb-I}$ overlaps.[40] Hence a single crystalline, distortion-free, and lower octahedral inclined ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ NRs formed along [110] direction with different diameter (112 and $157\mathrm{nm}$ ) is shown in Figure  3d. This is presumed to be responsible for obtaining high-performance, air-stable PV devices. To compare the crystallinity of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ NRs, we have further recorded SAED patterns of individual nanorod from as-deposited and annealed samples, which are presented in Figure S8, Supporting Information. The SAED pattern of annealed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ NR has been obtained from the white circle on the TEM image (Figure 3d), which indicates well-defined diffraction spots indexed to (010), (110), and (100) planes along [001] zone axis. The observation of sharper and clean diffraction spots (Figure S8, Supporting Information) of the annealed NR, as compared to the as-depo­ sited one, reveals the single crystalline nature of ${\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ NRs. However a weak reflection between the (100) and (110) spots in the annealed sample may arise from the presence of a finite fraction of orthorhombic phase $(\approx10\%)$ , because our XRD and Rietveld refinement analysis reveal that the annealed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ NR is the combination of tetragonal and orthorhombic phases and not a pure tetragonal one.
Figure  4a presents the optical absorbance spectra of thin films in γ (as-deposited) and $\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}$ ( $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ annealed) phases. Absorption features are observed around the UV to visible region and a strong peak observed around $590\mathrm{nm}$ can be attri­ buted to the transformation of nanocrystal to nanorods.[41] But in ${\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ , another broad hump is observed at around $620\mathrm{nm}$ that gradually saturates in the red region. The optical band gap of $\gamma$ and ${\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ phases are extracted from Tauc plots shown in Figure 4b. The estimated value of ${\beta\mathrm{{\cdotCsPbI}}_{3}}$ $(1.94\mathrm{eV})$ is found to be lower compared to $\gamma{\mathrm{cs}}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ $(2.03\mathrm{eV})$ due to the transition from orthorhombic to tetragonal phase.[42,43] The absor­bance spectrum of ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ NRs films remains unchanged even after 15 days under $45\text{\textperthousand}$ humidity condition, as shown in Figure 4a, indicating high air stability of annealed NRs.
We have studied the effect of annealing on the electronic band structure and DOS calculation of $\gamma$ and $\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}$ phases using density functional theory (DFT). The electronic band structure (Figure  4c,d) is presented for the Γ-X-R-Z-Γ-X-R-A-M-X paths in the first Brillouin zone. For $\gamma{\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ , the direct band gap occurs at the Γ-point of the Brillouin zone. On the other hand, the energy minima are shifted to the high-symmetry points of the Brillouin zone (Z-point) for ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ phase due to dyna­ mical Rashba effect.[44] DFT results show that ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ also has a direct band gap, which is essential for its use in optoelectronic and PV devices. The estimated band gap of ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ $(0.93{\mathrm{~eV}})$ is found to be lower than that of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CsPbI}}_{3}$ $(1.63\ \mathrm{eV})$ , which can be explained by the change of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{I}$ bond length.[45] The Pb-I bond length of ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ $(\mathrm{Pb-I}_{1}=3.11\mathrm{\mathring{A}}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}{\cdot}\mathrm{I}_{2}=3.04\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ is lower than that of $\gamma\mathrm{cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}$ $(\mathrm{Pb\mathbf{\cdot}I}_{1}=3.18\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}{\cdot}\mathrm{I}_{2}=3{\cdot}20\ \mathring\mathrm{A}$ ). The corresponding total density of states (t-DOS) and partial density of states $\mathrm{\dot{\boldsymbol{p}}}$ -DOS) projected on s-Pb, p-Pb, $\mathrm{{s{-l_{1}}}}$ , p-I1, $\mathbf{s}{}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{I}_{2}$ , $\left.\mathrm{p}\cdot\right|_{2}$ , s-Cs, and p-Cs are shown in Figure 4e,f and Figure S9a,b, Supporting Information. DOS curves clearly indicate that the band edge states are dominated by the p and s orbitals of I and $\mathrm{Pb}$ atoms and not from the Cs, because band edge states of Cs atom are far from the Fermi level.[46] In consequence, $\mathrm{PbI}_{6}$ octahedra rotations are stabilized in the valence band maxima (VBM) that is mainly contributed by the p-I orbital, and the conduction band minima (CBM) is dominated by the p-Pb orbital. In Figure ${4\mathrm{g}}\mathrm{.}$ , the optical absorption coefficient is plotted as a function of photon energy (frequency) for both γ and ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ phases, showing a significantly higher absorption for the latter in the visible region of electromagnetic spectrum. For ${\beta\mathrm{{\cdotCsPbI}}_{3}}$ , since the band gap is lower (as obtained experimentally), the absorption edge is shifted to the red region in the solar spectrum, which could be helpful as a better light harvesting material for PV applications. The band gap determined from DOS curve ( $1.63\ \mathrm{\eV}$ for $\mathsf{\gamma}_{\mathsf{X C S P b I}_{3}}$ and $0.93\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\beta\mathrm{{\cdotCsPbI}}_{3})$ is in excellent agreement with the photoabsorption edges at ${\approx}1.64~\mathrm{eV}$ for $\gamma{\mathrm{cs}}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ and ${\approx}0.94~\mathrm{eV}$ for ${\beta\mathrm{{\cdotCsPbI}}_{3}}$ .[47] However, the estimated band gaps from DFT are found to be underestimated as compared to the experimental data, which is also commonly reported in literature,[6,17,48,49] since the calculations are based on Predew–Burke–Ernzerhof generalized gradient approximation suggesting that further approximation is essential.
Finally, we have fabricated PV devices with inverted p-i-n configuration of $\mathrm{ITO/PEDOT{:}P S S/C s P b I_{3}/C_{60}/B C P/A l}$ and the details of the device fabrication are given in the Supporting Information. A cross-sectional FESEM micrograph of the fabricated device is shown in Figure S10, Supporting Information. To understand the energy band alignment of the perovskite solar cell, we performed UV photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS)
measurements, by using a He source emitting at an excitation energy of $21.6\mathrm{eV}_{:}$ , to obtain the work function (WF) and valence band maxima. Figure 5a shows the secondary cut-off region at 17.87 and 17.91  eV for $\gamma$ and ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ NR films, respectively. The estimated WF is found to be 3.75 and $3.69~\mathrm{eV}$ for $\gamma$ and ${\beta\mathrm{{\cdotCsPbI}}_{3}}$ NRs, respectively, which is in good agreement with previously reported values.[6] On the other hand, the VBM (for $\beta\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3})$ is shifted by about $0.29\ \mathrm{eV}$ towards a higher binding energy as compared to $\gamma{\mathrm{cs}}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ sample, which is illustrated in Figure  5b. From the extracted values of WF, VBM position (relative to the Fermi level) and using the reported band gap for the materials $(2.65\ \mathrm{eV}$ for PEDOT:PSS,[50] $1.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ ,[51] and $3.5~\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathsf{B C P}^{[52]},$ ), the proposed energy band diagram of the fabricated inverted PV device is presented Figure  5c. The deposited $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ NRs film behaves as n-type semiconductors because the Fermi level is closer to the conduction band. Therefore, the conduction band offset (CBO) for ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ layer (0.41  eV) is closer to the Fermi level than that of $\gamma\mathrm{cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ one $(0.79\mathrm{eV})$ , which may lead to easier transport of photo-generated electrons in the former. On the other hand, valance band maxima in $\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}$ $(-5.22~\mathrm{eV})$ lies below that of $\gamma{\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ $(-4.99~\mathrm{eV})$ , which is above the VBM of hole transport layer (HTL). So the band alignment for $\varkappa\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ layer is not suitable for efficient hole injection into the HTL, as shown in Figure 5c. In contrast, a favorable valence band alignment in ${\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ is beneficial for improved hole transport. So a higher open circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ of $960~\mathrm{mV}$ has been achieved for annealed $\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}$ NRs due to higher crystalline quality, lesser grain boundaries, and lower optical band gap. On the other hand, a favorable band alignment leads to the improvement in shortcircuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ from 9.16 to $11.34\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ NRs, as shown in the current density−voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ characteristics in (Figure  5e). A significant enhancement in fill factor (FF) from $59.6\%$ to $67.2\%$ for ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ is attributed to a lower series resistance $R_{\mathrm{S}}$ of $8.34~\Omega~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ in comparison to $14.77\ \Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ for $\varkappa\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ . An overall PCE of $7.3\%$ is achieved for ${\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ NRs as compared to $4.1\%$ in $\gamma\mathrm{cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ showing an enhancement by $78\%$ in the former. To further characterize the performance of IPSC devices, we have performed external quantum efficiency (EQE) measurements and calculated integrated $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ values. The estimated current density in $J{-}V$ curve agrees well with the integrated current density extracted from EQE, which is shown in Figure  5d. It is clear, that the EQE response of ${\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ -based device is superior to the $\gamma{\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ based one, due to the enhanced light absorption in the visible spectrum. The overall performance of ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ -based PV device including the cell parameters, viz., FF, and efficiency are found to be enhanced. However, we noticed a strong hysteresis effect in forward and reverse scans in the ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ based IPSCs, as shown in Figure S11, Supporting Information. This kind of hysteresis behavior has also been reported earlier in perovskite solar cells.[53,54] The PV parameters of air stable IPSCs are summarized in Table 1, which shows that shunt resistance ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ needs to be improved further. It may be noted that a further optimization of device fabrication process needs to be done with proper choice of electrodes to minimize the series resistance, charge transport layers, contact metallizations, and surface passivation, which will lead to achieve much higher efficiency in ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ NR-based solar cells. The performance of our colloidal synthesized ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ NR-based PV device is compared with those previously reported in the literature using $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ nanocrystals of different phases under varying humidity conditions in Table  2. It may be noted the device area is also a critical parameter related to the efficiency, which was the highest in our case. Our results indicate the great potential of ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ NRs in laboratory humidity conditions for future lowcost, air-stable, all inorganic perovskite-based PV devices.
## 3. Conclusions
In conclusion, we have reported that air-stable $\beta\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}$ NRs could be obtained using a novel colloidal synthesis route followed by post-growth annealing of relatively unstable $\gamma{\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ thin films at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Rietveld refinement indicates that the annealing causes the movement of atoms inside the unit cell resulting in distortion free $\mathrm{PbI}_{6}$ octahedra, which could be visualized through XRD structure, HRTEM image analyses and DFT calculations. Formed ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ NR-based thin films are found to be single crystalline in nature with perfectly oriented
NRs exhibiting improved structural stability in comparison to as-deposited $\gamma{\mathrm{{CsPbI}}_{3}}$ due to distortion free unit cell. DFTbased calculations have been utilized to calculate the electronic band structure and nature of bandgap of ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ phase. This high-quality, single-crystalline ${\beta\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C s P b I}}_{3}}$ NRs thin films not only underscore the performance of PV devices with a moderately efficiency $(>7\%)$ but also provide highly stable colloidal synthesis of perovskite layers under $45\text{\textperthousand}$ humid condition for potential use of optoelectronic devices in future.
